# The Linux personality, in a domain of its own -- RFC 0032 step 3, and the
# first time RFC 0005's "a service domain, not the nucleus" has been true of
# anything.
#
# **This list is the whole of its authority**, and it is what makes the
# containment claim checkable rather than asserted: a bug in the largest
# untrusted-input parser this project will ever have reaches exactly what is
# written here. It said "one endpoint, and nothing else -- not even a console"
# until 2026-08-20, when RFC 0032 step 10 moved the last two Linux calls out of
# the nucleus and each needed the capability the kernel had been using on a
# hosted program's behalf:
#
#   - two pages, to report through and to hand a faulting program's registers
#     over in;
#   - the console. The grant is **write-only** -- it may put a character and
#     may not take a byte somebody typed at the shell -- but this grammar has
#     no way to say so, so the line below claims *more* authority than the
#     domain has. That is the safe direction for a reviewer to be wrong in,
#     and the trigger for a `cap console` is the first package that is
#     installed rather than started by the kernel and needs the narrower one;
#   - sixteen notifications, which it may signal and may not wait on: one per
#     hosted thread asleep in a `futex`, which is also the limit on how many
#     may sleep at once.
#
# **Two of those three cannot be written in this grammar, and that is a finding
# rather than an omission.** `cap console` cannot say *write-only*, and there
# is no way at all to say *sixteen notifications*: `MAX_CAPS` is sixteen
# "distinct authorities", and sixteen identical notifications are one authority
# with a multiplicity, which the memory line already has a spelling for
# (`pages=`). So the notifications are described here and not listed, because
# twelve `cap notification` lines would be a *wrong* list where a paragraph is
# an honest one. The trigger for `cap notification count=N` and
# `cap console write` is the first package that is **installed** rather than
# started by the kernel and needs either -- these grants are made by
# `start_linux_domain` at boot, so nothing is granted from this file today.
#
# A supervisor handle to each domain it hosts is granted by the kernel as
# hosted domains appear, and is not a fixed grant. When it grows a hosted
# process's files and sockets it will hold *those*, and a reviewer will see
# them on this list.
#
# **That day has a date on it now.** RFC 0033 (`what a hosted process is`,
# drafted 2026-08-20) proposes giving this domain `DomainControl` and a
# directory capability so it can serve `execve` and files -- the largest grant
# it will have asked for, and the one that most needs to be written on a list a
# reviewer reads. The grammar gaps above stop being an inconvenience there: a
# list that cannot say *write-only*, cannot say *sixteen*, and would have to
# say *a subtree of the filesystem* is not yet the reviewable artifact RFC 0030
# claims. Fixing the grammar is part of that RFC's step, not a follow-up.
package linuxd
version 0.1.0

program bin/linuxd
entry hertz
cap serve linux
cap memory pages=1
cap memory pages=1
cap console

payload bin/linuxd from user/linuxd/target/x86_64-unknown-none/release/linuxd
